    description: "Larry Ellison is the co-founder and longtime CEO of Oracle Corporation — the world's second-largest software company by revenue and the dominant force in enterprise database software for four decades. He dropped out of two universities (the University of Illinois and the University of Chicago) before working as a computer programmer, eventually co-founding Oracle in 1977 with two partners. His key insight was that Edgar Codd's relational database model — then purely theoretical — could be built into a commercial product, and Oracle's relational database became the backbone of enterprise computing worldwide. His estimated IQ of 145 reflects extraordinary technical insight combined with ruthless commercial instincts and a relentless competitive drive that earned him a reputation as one of the most formidable and aggressive business competitors in Silicon Valley.",
    iqContext: "Ellison's estimated IQ of 145 reflects high giftedness across technical and strategic domains, combined with the kind of intense competitive disposition that cognitive assessments do not measure. His famous competitiveness — expressed in his America's Cup racing (Oracle Team USA won consecutively), his public feuds with competitors, and his management style — has been as important to Oracle's success as his technical insight. His career illustrates a pattern seen in several technology billionaires: formal education abandoned in favor of hands-on technical work, followed by the kind of commercial success that retrospectively makes the dropout seem like prescience rather than failure. His friendship with Steve Jobs — a fellow competitive, design-obsessed college dropout — was one of Silicon Valley's most consequential personal relationships.",
