    iqContext: "The wide range of Lincoln's IQ estimate (128–150) reflects genuine scholarly uncertainty: his cognitive profile was highly distinctive, with extraordinary verbal and rhetorical ability alongside practical political genius, but without the formal educational credentials that typically allow more precise comparison. His prose style — the Gettysburg Address in particular — is considered among the finest in the English language and reflects a command of rhythm, parallelism, and conceptual compression that is the hallmark of very high verbal intelligence. His management of his cabinet — which included rivals, critics, and men who considered themselves his superior — is a masterclass in political intelligence. His depression, which contemporaries noted and which he called the 'hypo,' is a recurring feature of discussion about his psychological profile.",
